Using `collections.OrderedDict` is an efficient way to implement an LRU cache because it maintains insertion order. When an item is accessed or added, it is moved to the end of the dictionary. When the cache exceeds its capacity, the item at the beginning (the oldest) is popped.

```python
from collections import OrderedDict

class LRUCache:
    def __init__(self, capacity):
        self.cache = OrderedDict()
        self.capacity = capacity

    def get(self, key):
        if key not in self.cache: return -1
        self.cache.move_to_end(key)
        return self.cache[key]

    def put(self, key, value):
        if key in self.cache: self.cache.move_to_end(key)
        self.cache[key] = value
        if len(self.cache) > self.capacity:
            self.cache.popitem(last=False)
```
Both `get` and `put` operations have an average time complexity of O(1) due to the underlying hash map and doubly-linked list structure of the OrderedDict.

